          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Most computer models of soil carbon — the kind used to project how much carbon will leave soils as the climate warms or as land use changes — are calibrated against laboratory studies of bare soil, with no living plants present. Soil microbial ecologist Wei Cheng has argued that this is a serious blind spot. In a 2014 synthesis paper, Cheng compiled experimental measurements of the rhizosphere priming effect, the documented tendency of live plant roots to stimulate soil microbes to decompose previously stable organic matter. He concluded that because mainstream soil-carbon models leave the priming effect out of their equations, they systematically underestimate the amount of carbon those soils will release under elevated atmospheric carbon dioxide and under shifts to more productive vegetation. The implication is that the same models on which carbon-budget projections are built may be reading future soils as more stable than they actually are.",
