          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Although Olduvai Gorge in northern Tanzania had been known to scientists since the early twentieth century, paleoanthropologist Mary Leakey's 1971 monograph on her excavations in Beds I and II changed how the site was used as evidence. Earlier accounts had treated the gorge primarily as a source of striking individual fossils, sometimes reported without careful attention to which sediment layer each specimen had come from. Leakey instead documented the gorge as a layered sequence of dated deposits and recorded, for each excavated layer, both the stone tools recovered there and the fossil hominin remains associated with the same deposit. By assembling tools and bones layer by layer, she produced something that earlier reports had not: a record in which a particular industry of stone toolmaking could be linked directly to the hominins found alongside it, rather than treated as a free-floating cultural fact.",
