          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Bioarchaeologist Kristina Killgrove and her collaborators have used stable isotope analysis of bone collagen to study what people in Imperial-era Rome actually ate. Stable isotope ratios of carbon and nitrogen preserved in collagen reflect the long-term mix of plant and animal foods, including the share of marine resources, in an individual's diet. In a 2014 study of skeletal remains from cemeteries serving the urban poor, Killgrove's team reported a striking pattern: the proportion of marine-derived nitrogen in the sampled bones was lower than the textual sources, which describe broad public distributions of fish sauce and ocean fish, would suggest. Killgrove argued that diet in Imperial Rome was not the uniform, marine-rich diet implied by the literature on its food trade but instead varied with social status, with marine resources concentrated among wealthier residents and largely absent from the poorer suburbs the cemeteries served.",
