          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In Whitewalling: Art, Race, and Protest in 3 Acts, the critic Aruna D'Souza walks through three high-profile American museum controversies — over works by Dana Schutz, Sam Durant, and Donelle Woolford — in which Black artists, writers, and visitors objected on the record to images of Black suffering produced and exhibited by non-Black artists. In every one of the three episodes, D'Souza shows, the institution heard those objections, weighed the choice in front of it, and proceeded with the work in place by appealing to a commitment to artistic freedom and curatorial neutrality. D'Souza's reading of the three cases together therefore implies that, when an American museum frames a controversy of this kind as a contest between artistic neutrality and community objection, ______",
