          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In interviews accompanying the 2016 retrospective Kerry James Marshall: Mastry, the painter Kerry James Marshall set out a position about the genre of history painting itself. The Western canon had largely treated history painting — the genre by which museums certified what counted as a serious public subject — as a tradition that simply did not contain dark-skinned figures, and Marshall's project was to claim that genre rather than abandon it: he renders his subjects, who are almost always Black, in a deliberately unmodulated black pigment that refuses any softening toward the lighter skin tones the canon had taken as the default. Marshall's account of his own practice therefore implies that, for an artist who shares his commitment to remaking history painting around Black figuration, ______",
