          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In a 2019 study of nineteenth-century natural-history publishing, the historian of science Hugh Torrens revisited the career of Mary Anning, the fossil collector who worked the Jurassic cliffs of Lyme Regis on the southern coast of England in the early nineteenth century. Anning's articulated ichthyosaur and plesiosaur specimens entered the scientific record largely through papers written by male geologists, since the Geological Society of London did not admit women as members until 1919. Torrens argues that the apparent unevenness of Anning's credit in the published record reflects not her contemporaries' personal regard for her, which their private correspondence shows was often high, but the institutional convention that only Society members could appear as authors of formal scientific papers, a convention that quietly determined whose name reached print.",
