          "skill": "transitions",
          "passage": "Through the early twentieth century, leading developmental psychologists in the United States held that the emotional turbulence and intergenerational conflict associated with adolescence were biological in origin — built into the maturation of the human body and therefore essentially the same in every society. ______ in her 1928 ethnography Coming of Age in Samoa, the cultural anthropologist Margaret Mead reported that the young women she lived among on the island of Ta'ū moved into adulthood with relatively little of the storm-and-stress reaction the American clinical literature had treated as universal, suggesting that the social setting in which adolescence unfolds shapes the experience as much as biology does.",
