          "skill": "transitions",
          "passage": "By the late twentieth century, North American political theorists writing on Indigenous peoples and the settler state had largely converged on the position that meaningful political standing for nations such as the Mohawk could be secured only through formal recognition by Canadian and U.S. legal institutions — that is, by working the existing apparatus of treaties, courts, and citizenship rules from the inside until acknowledgment was extended. ______ in her 2014 book Mohawk Interruptus, the Kahnawà:ke political anthropologist Audra Simpson had quietly built up, from years of fieldwork on her own Mohawk reserve and on the long-running Kahnawà:ke practice of declining U.S. and Canadian passports, a precise counter-argument: that the everyday Mohawk refusal to be recognized on settler terms is itself a coherent assertion of political life, and that taking such recognition as the only valid pathway already concedes the very sovereignty Indigenous nations are trying to retain.",
