          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Willa Cather's 1913 novel \"O Pioneers!\". Alexandra Bergson, a young Swedish American woman who has just inherited her family's farm, has driven up to a high ridge above the Nebraska prairie.\n\nFor the first time, perhaps, since that land emerged from the waters of geologic ages, a human face was set toward it with love and yearning. It seemed beautiful to her, rich and strong and glorious. Her eyes drank in the breadth of it, until her tears blinded her. Then the Genius of the Divide, the great, free spirit which breathes across it, must have bent lower than it ever bent to a human will before. The history of every country begins in the heart of a man or a woman. Alexandra had never known before how much the country meant to her. The chirping of the insects down in the long grass had been like the sweetest music. She had felt as if her heart were hiding down there, with the quail and the plover and all the little wild things that crooned or buzzed in the sun. Under the long shaggy ridges, she felt the future stirring.",
          "question": "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?",
