          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Anton Chekhov's 1899 short story \"The Lady with the Dog,\" translated from the Russian. Dmitri Gurov, a Moscow banker on holiday at the seaside resort of Yalta, has begun to take notice of an unfamiliar young woman walking along the promenade.\n\nIt was said that a new person had appeared on the sea-front: a lady with a little dog. Dmitri Dmitritch Gurov, who had by then been a fortnight at Yalta, and so was fairly at home there, had begun to take an interest in new arrivals. Sitting in Verney's pavilion, he saw, walking on the sea-front, a fair-haired young lady of medium height, wearing a beret; a white Pomeranian dog was running behind her. And afterwards he met her in the public gardens and in the square several times a day. She was walking alone, always wearing the same beret, and always with the same white dog; no one knew who she was, and every one called her simply \"the lady with the dog.\" \"If she is here alone without a husband or friends,\" Gurov reflected, \"it wouldn't be amiss to make her acquaintance.\"",
          "question": "Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?",
