          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Text 2 makes its partial-agreement structure explicit: Pielke \"agrees with Huq that vulnerable countries need expanded adaptation finance and accepts the policy goal\" of supporting countries already absorbing major climate-driven harms. What he contests is a specific empirical claim on which Huq's framing depends — that present-day disaster losses can be confidently attributed to anthropogenic emissions rather than to growth in population, infrastructure, and asset values in exposed regions. Once damages are normalized to changes in exposure, in his analyses, the climate-attributable residual is small relative to the exposure-driven trend. Crucially, the conclusion he draws is methodological, not anti-policy: \"the policy goal is not wrong\" but the evidentiary basis for the loss-and-damage framing as a settled debt is weaker than the rhetoric suggests, and adaptation policy should foreground vulnerability reduction. Choice D captures exactly this targeted-attribution objection inside a shared policy frame.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: surface match — A casts Pielke as disputing Huq's account of adaptation in Bangladesh — embankments, salt-tolerant rice, migration corridors. Text 2 says nothing of the kind; Pielke's quarrel is with the attribution accounting for normalized disaster losses, not with the description of what Bangladesh has already implemented. A substitutes a fact-of-the-ground objection for the actual evidentiary one.\n- Choice B: scope shift — B reframes the dispute as a theoretical claim that international negotiating frameworks cannot in principle adjudicate distributive claims that arise within national systems. Nothing in Text 2 raises a sovereignty-of-scale argument; Pielke's intervention is empirical and concerns the documentary record on attribution, not the institutional capacity of UNFCCC processes. This is precisely the technical/scaling scope-shift trap §2.2 warns against.\n- Choice C: inverse — C inverts Pielke's actual position by concluding that, because the climate-attributable share is small in his analyses, no obligation to provide adaptation finance arises and emitters should be released from loss-and-damage commitments. Text 2 says the opposite: the policy goal of adaptation finance is accepted; what is contested is only the evidentiary basis for the specific historical-debt framing. C pushes a targeted methodological objection into a categorical denial of obligation Pielke explicitly avoids.",
