          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Imported glass beads and cowrie shells turn up in great quantities at archaeological sites across Yorubaland in present-day Nigeria, especially at sites occupied during the era of intensive Atlantic trade. A common assumption has been that such finds passively register the volume and direction of European commerce, with their value in Yorubaland tracking their value in the Atlantic system that produced them. Archaeologist Akinwumi Ogundiran has challenged this view. Drawing on excavated assemblages alongside oral traditions and ritual practice, Ogundiran has argued that beads and cowries entering Yoruba towns were absorbed into existing local frameworks of meaning: they were used to mark divinity, to organize lineage and political rank, and to serve as ritual offerings. Their significance in Yorubaland, Ogundiran contends, was set by these local cultural translations rather than by the commercial logic of the Atlantic trade through which they had arrived.",
