          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The passage sets up a common assumption — that imported beads and cowries passively register Atlantic commerce, with their Yoruba value tracking their Atlantic value — and then frames Ogundiran's contribution as a challenge to that assumption. Drawing on excavated assemblages, oral traditions, and ritual practice, Ogundiran argues that beads and cowries were absorbed into local frameworks: marking divinity, organizing lineage and rank, and serving as ritual offerings. The closing sentence restates the synthesizing claim: their significance was set by local cultural translations rather than by Atlantic commercial logic. Choice D captures this central argument at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: inverse — A is the position the passage explicitly identifies as the \"common assumption\" Ogundiran has challenged. Choosing A treats the view Ogundiran rejects as the view the passage attributes to him.\n- Choice B: scope shift — the passage lists oral traditions as one of three lines of evidence Ogundiran draws on, alongside excavated assemblages and ritual practice. B promotes one item from that list to \"the only reliable evidence,\" overstating Ogundiran's claim.\n- Choice C: surface match — \"sites occupied during the era of intensive Atlantic trade\" appears in the first sentence, which makes a chronological claim about site occupation feel topically right. The passage makes no claim that the largest concentrations come from sites occupied before that era; C imports a quantitative chronological claim the passage does not assert.",
