          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Patterns of dental wear and tooth disease in human skeletons can record what people ate and how they prepared their food, because the abrasiveness of stone-ground grain, the texture of cooked versus raw foods, and the sugar content of the diet all leave distinctive marks on enamel. Bioarchaeologist Yilmaz Selim Erdal has applied this principle to skeletal samples spanning the long Neolithic occupation of Çatalhöyük in central Anatolia. In a 2015 study, Erdal documented a gradual change in the dental record across that occupation: heavy enamel wear of the kind associated with coarse stone-ground cereals lessened in later phases, while patterns consistent with softer, more processed foods became more common. He further reported that, within the same household samples, the dental wear patterns of adult males and adult females diverged more sharply over time, suggesting that food preparation and food access had become more differentiated by sex within Çatalhöyük households.",
