          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The passage names two findings of Erdal's 2015 Çatalhöyük study and ties them to a synthesizing interpretation. The first finding is a gradual change in the dental record across the occupation, with heavy wear from coarse stone-ground cereals lessening and patterns consistent with softer, more processed foods becoming more common. The second is that within the same households, male and female dental wear diverged more sharply over time. The closing clause makes the interpretation explicit: changes in food preparation and growing sex-based differentiation in food access. Choice B captures both findings and the joint interpretation at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: inverse — A reverses one of the two findings. The passage says heavy enamel wear of the kind associated with coarse stone-ground cereals lessened in later phases; A says it remained essentially unchanged.\n- Choice C: scope shift — the first sentence establishes the methodological principle that wear and disease patterns record diet and food preparation. C lifts that setup and treats it as the main idea, ignoring the actual findings the passage attributes to the 2015 study.\n- Choice D: surface match — migration-based readings of bioarchaeological divergence are common in the field, so a migration explanation for the male/female divergence sounds plausible. But the passage attributes the divergence to differentiation of food preparation and access within households, not to long-distance migration; D imports an explanation not in the passage.",
