          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Late Neolithic stone-built funerary monuments are scattered across the dry interior of southeastern Mauritania, and earlier surveys had cataloged them mainly as a typology — sorting cairns, tumuli, and platform graves by shape and assigning each form a notional cultural affiliation. In a 1993 study, archaeologist Augustin Holl proposed reading the same monuments differently. Rather than asking what each monument type was, he treated the full set as a spatial dataset and analyzed how the monuments were distributed across the landscape: their distances from one another, their alignments along seasonal water routes, and their clustering around specific pasture zones. He acknowledged that this spatial reading does not by itself fix the cultural identity of any single monument or determine its precise date. Even so, Holl argued, the recurrent patterning across the region — too regular to be accidental — points to an organized pastoral society whose movements and territorial claims structured how the monuments were placed, even before any one tomb is securely dated or attributed.",
