          "passage": "When the anthropologist Zora Neale Hurston published Tell My Horse in 1938, she presented her account of Vodou in Haiti against a background of foreign travel writing that had usually rendered the religion as sensational primitivism. Hurston, who had trained at Barnard College and conducted extended fieldwork in rural Haiti, instead described the practice in the categories anthropologists used for any complex religion: she traced its theology, set out the ethical obligations it imposed on practitioners, and documented the political functions its temples carried in the surrounding community. The contrast Hurston draws between her own account and the earlier travel-writing tradition therefore implies that those earlier writers had ______",
