          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In Patterns of Culture (1934), the anthropologist Ruth Benedict advanced what she called configurational analysis: each culture, she argued, selects from the vast range of possible human behaviors a coherent configuration, so an individual personality becomes intelligible only against the patterned whole of the society in which it forms. To illustrate the point, she contrasted three societies — Pueblo, Plains, and Dobuan — and argued that a temperament prized as healthy in one configuration could appear, against the patterned whole of another, as a marked deviation. Benedict's argument therefore implies that an investigator who applies a single, society-independent diagnostic standard to behaviors observed across these three settings ______",
