          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "When Ella Cara Deloria, a trained linguist and a member of the Yankton Sioux community, prepared Dakota Texts (1932), she transcribed her source narratives in the original Dakota language and printed them alongside English translations. In her introduction, Deloria argued that the narratives were not entertainments alone: in their oral performance, particular grammatical forms, kinship terms of address, and patterned repetitions were doing the work of legal and moral instruction for the listeners. Translating those narratives into English, she observed, could carry over what the stories were about, but it could not by itself preserve the features through which the instruction was actually delivered. Deloria's account of how Dakota oral narratives carry pedagogical force therefore implies that a researcher who works only from English summaries of these stories ______",
