          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In a 2021 essay on the history of dark-matter astronomy, the science writer Lisa Randall returned to the spectroscopic measurements that the astronomer Vera Rubin and her collaborator Kent Ford carried out at the Carnegie Institution of Washington in the late 1960s and 1970s. Rubin and Ford recorded how rapidly stars at successive distances from the centers of spiral galaxies move, and they found that the rotational velocities of stars near the visible edge of a disk did not fall off as the visible mass distribution predicted. Randall argues that the special force of Rubin and Ford's result was not its initial novelty — anomalous rotation curves had been reported in isolated cases earlier — but the breadth of their survey, since the same flat profile recurred across many galaxies of different sizes and types, leaving no room for the discrepancy to be dismissed as a peculiarity of any single object.",
